12:["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"topic":{"id":"burnout-and-stress","title":"Burnout & Chronic Stress: The Cost of Running Without Recovery","subtitle":"Exhaustion, cynicism, and dwindling effectiveness — and how to reverse them.","category":"Burnout & Stress","tone":"sand","icon":"batteryLow","keywords":["burnout","stress","overwork","exhaustion","boundaries","cynicism"],"readingTime":7,"featuredImage":"/bc248231-c940-4e95-a939-9f1a54a31f7a.jpg","excerpt":"Burnout is an occupational condition with three measurable dimensions. Rest alone will not fix it — the demands and the recovery both have to change.","reviewer":"Reviewed by Dr. Marcus Oyelaran, MD","updated":"Updated August 2026","intro":["Burnout is not simply being tired. The World Health Organization defines it as a syndrome from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed, with three parts: exhaustion, mental distance or cynicism, and reduced effectiveness.","Naming all three matters. People often try to solve burnout with a holiday, then feel defeated when the cynicism returns in week one. Exhaustion responds to rest; the other two respond to changes in demand, control, and meaning."],"mechanics":{"heading":"Why a weekend does not reset it","body":["Under sustained load, cortisol regulation shifts. Recovery becomes slower and less complete, so each week starts from a deficit. The body adapts by lowering engagement — that flatness is protective, not lazy.","Genuine recovery needs both a reduction in demand and active restoration: sleep, movement, connection, and time where no one needs anything from you. One without the other stalls."]},"symptoms":[{"label":"Sunday-night dread","description":"Anticipatory tension that arrives before the week does.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Tension headaches and jaw clenching","description":"Muscular holding patterns that persist into evenings and sleep.","kind":"Body"},{"label":"Compassion fatigue","description":"Caring feels expensive, so distance replaces it — often with guilt attached.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Task paralysis","description":"Staring at a short list, unable to start, then working late to compensate.","kind":"Behavior"}],"myths":[{"myth":"Burnout means you are not resilient enough.","fact":"Burnout is a property of workload and conditions, not of character. Resilience training alone does not fix an unmanageable job."},{"myth":"A vacation will fix burnout.","fact":"Time off relieves exhaustion temporarily. Without changes to demand or control, symptoms typically return within weeks."},{"myth":"Only caring professions burn out.","fact":"It occurs in any role with high demand and low autonomy, including creative, technical, and unpaid caregiving work."}],"strategies":[{"name":"Boundary script templates","summary":"Pre-written language makes saying no possible in the moment.","steps":["“I can take this on if we move X — which would you prefer?”","“I am at capacity this week. I can start Monday.”","“I am offline after 18:30. I will pick this up first thing.”","Send without apologising or over-explaining."]},{"name":"Recovery audit","summary":"Distinguishes real restoration from numbing that leaves you tired.","steps":["For one week, log what you do after work and how you feel an hour later.","Mark each entry restorative, neutral, or depleting.","Replace one depleting habit with a restorative one at the same time slot."]},{"name":"Shutdown ritual","summary":"Gives the nervous system a clear end-of-day signal.","steps":["Write tomorrow’s three priorities before closing anything.","Close every tab and app, out loud if it helps.","Change clothes or walk once around the block to mark the transition."]}],"treatments":[{"name":"Cognitive Behavioural Therapy","abbreviation":"CBT","description":"Addresses perfectionism, over-responsibility, and the beliefs that make boundaries feel dangerous."},{"name":"Acceptance & Commitment Therapy","abbreviation":"ACT","description":"Clarifies values so work choices stop being driven by obligation, and builds tolerance for the discomfort of saying no."},{"name":"Occupational health review","abbreviation":"OH","description":"A clinician can document capacity, recommend adjustments, and rule out anaemia, thyroid issues, or sleep apnoea."}],"support":{"guidance":"Resist the urge to optimise their calendar. Someone in burnout has already heard every productivity tip. Offer to absorb load instead, and be specific about what you will take.","say":["“What is one thing I can take off your plate this week?”","“You are allowed to be this tired.”","“I will handle dinner and the school run Thursday.”"],"avoid":["“Everyone is busy right now.”","“Maybe you just need better time management.”","“At least you have a job.”"]},"faqs":[{"question":"Is burnout a medical diagnosis?","answer":"It is classified as an occupational phenomenon rather than a medical condition, but it frequently coexists with depression or anxiety, which are diagnosable and treatable."},{"question":"Do I need to quit my job?","answer":"Rarely the first step. Changes to workload, autonomy, and recovery resolve many cases. If conditions cannot change, a longer-term plan may be needed."},{"question":"How long does recovery take?","answer":"Mild burnout often improves in weeks once demands drop. Severe, long-standing burnout commonly takes several months of sustained change."}],"pacer":[{"label":"Box breathing","inhale":4,"hold":4,"exhale":4,"description":"Steady and repeatable — good between meetings or before a difficult conversation."},{"label":"Long 4-2-8 exhale","inhale":4,"hold":2,"exhale":8,"description":"For the end of the day, when the body needs a clear signal to stand down."}]}}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}]
